WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:05.050
In this lesson we will look at another method
for solving systems

2
00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:08.890
of linear algebraic equations: Gaussian
elimination.

3
00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:16.570
Let a system of m linear algebraic equations
in n unknowns be given. Whether

4
00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:19.877
the number of equations equals the number of
unknowns

5
00:00:19.877 --> 00:00:22.810
or not, to solve the system of linear
equations

6
00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:26.470
we can use Gaussian elimination.

7
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:32.240
Let us write the augmented matrix of the
system.

8
00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:40.900
Gaussian elimination has two stages. The first
stage, the forward pass: at this

9
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:44.324
stage we bring the augmented matrix to echelon
form.

10
00:00:44.324 --> 00:00:47.550
At the second stage we write the resulting
matrix

11
00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:54.260
as a system and solve it by eliminating
variables: starting from the last variable,

12
00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:59.900
we find all the other variables. This is
called the backward pass.

13
00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:06.812
Let us look at an example: we need to solve
the system by Gaussian

14
00:01:06.812 --> 00:01:11.340
elimination. Let us write the augmented
matrix. First, a₁₁

15
00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:14.423
must not be zero; this is a necessary
condition.

16
00:01:14.423 --> 00:01:17.570
But to make the calculations easier, it is
better

17
00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:26.070
for a₁₁ to equal one, so we swap the first and
fourth rows.

18
00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:31.616
We multiply the entries of the first row by
minus

19
00:01:31.616 --> 00:01:34.630
five, three and minus two and add them

20
00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:39.555
to the entries of the second, third and fourth
rows respectively.

21
00:01:39.555 --> 00:01:43.040
Then the entries below a₁₁ in the first column

22
00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:49.780
become zero. At the second step, for the new
matrix,

23
00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:56.540
first, a₂₂ must not be zero; this is a
necessary condition. But

24
00:01:56.540 --> 00:02:03.300
to make the calculations easier, it is better
for a₂₂ to equal one or minus one,

25
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:06.939
so we swap the second and third rows.

26
00:02:06.939 --> 00:02:13.430
Now we multiply the entries of the second row
by four and by three

27
00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:19.720
and add them to the entries of the third and
fourth rows respectively.

28
00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:27.860
Then the entries below a₂₂ in the second
column become zero.

29
00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:33.067
In the resulting matrix a₃₃ equals twenty-six,

30
00:02:33.067 --> 00:02:36.150
so we multiply the entries of the third

31
00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:40.692
row by minus twenty-four and the entries of
the fourth row by

32
00:02:40.692 --> 00:02:45.010
twenty-six, and add them to the entries of the
fourth row.

33
00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:52.990
This gives an echelon matrix. The
corresponding system looks like this.

34
00:02:54.820 --> 00:03:01.580
From the last equation, x₄ equals one. From
the third equation we find x₃; it

35
00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:08.270
equals zero. From the second equation we find
x₂; it equals seven. From the first

36
00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:11.850
equation we found x₁; it equals five.

37
00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:20.060
So the unknowns equal five, seven, zero and
one.

38
00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:26.025
So, to sum up: Gaussian elimination is a very
efficient method

39
00:03:26.025 --> 00:03:28.900
for solving systems of linear algebraic
equations.

40
00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:31.018
Compared with other methods,

41
00:03:31.018 --> 00:03:35.860
it has a wide range of use: there is no need
to study the system

42
00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:37.974
for consistency beforehand.

43
00:03:37.974 --> 00:03:42.750
Gaussian elimination works when the main
determinant is zero,

44
00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:46.842
when the number of unknowns matches the number

45
00:03:46.842 --> 00:03:50.490
of equations, and when they do not match.
